## The Bug

The condition explicitly rejects `null`, `undefined`, and `false`, but **never checks for an empty string**. Since `""` is not strictly equal to `null`, `undefined`, or `false`, it passes all three comparisons and returns `true`.

```javascript
"" !== null      // true  → passes check
"" !== undefined // true  → passes check
"" !== false     // true  → passes check (strict comparison, different types)
```

