## The Bug

This is the classic **closure loop variable capture** problem. The anonymous function captures `i` by reference — all goroutines share the *same* variable `i`. By the time the goroutines actually run, the loop has finished and `i` is already `5` (the value that caused the loop to exit).

## The Fix

Pass `i` as a parameter to the goroutine function. This creates a new copy of the value for each goroutine:

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			fmt.Println(i)
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

## Note

Starting with **Go 1.22**, the semantics changed — each loop iteration gets its own variable, so the original code would work without the parameter trick. But since you're on Go 1.21, the explicit pass-by-parameter fix is the standard approach.

