message.content:
## Diagnosis

The root cause is **integer overflow**, compounded by Rust's different overflow behavior between debug and release profiles:

| Mode | Overflow Behavior |
|------|------------------|
| **Debug** (`cargo run`) | Overflow **panics** — you see an error immediately |
| **Release** (`cargo run --release`) | Overflow **wraps silently** (two's complement) — no error, just a wrong number |

`factorial(21)` = 51,090,942,171,709,440,000 which already exceeds `u64::MAX` (18,446,744,073,709,551,615). So `factorial(25)` overflows at the 21st multiplication. In debug mode you'd see a panic (which the user may not have run), but in release mode the wrapped value is returned silently.

## Fix

Use `checked_mul` so overflow is detected regardless of build profile:

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn factorial(n: u64) -> Option<u64> {
    let mut result: u64 = 1;
    for i in 1..=n {
        result = result.checked_mul(i)?;
    }
    Some(result)
}

fn main() {
    match factorial(20) {
        Some(v) => println!("{}", v),
        None => println!("overflow!"),
    }
    match factorial(25) {
        Some(v) => println!("{}", v),
        None => println!("overflow!"),
    }
}
</solution>

`checked_mul` returns `None` when the multiplication would overflow, making the overflow explicit in **both** debug and release builds. The `?` operator propagates `None` out of the function, so callers must handle the overflow case — no more silent wrong answers.

