Looking at this with input `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1]`, I can trace the issues:

**Bug 1 (line 7):** `data[i] == max_val` compares the current element against the *best value found so far* rather than the *previous element in the sequence*. This means once `max_val` is set to `2`, the `1`s will never be recognized as a new streak — they'll just keep resetting `current_count` to `1`. It should be `data[i] == data[i - 1]`.

**Bug 2 (after the loop):** There's no check after the loop to handle the case where the *last* streak is the longest. With `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1]`, the streak of `1`s runs to the end of the array and never triggers the `else` branch that would compare it against `max_count`.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == data[i - 1] {
            current_count += 1;
        } else {
            if current_count > max_count {
                max_count = current_count;
                max_val = data[i - 1];
            }
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = data[data.len() - 1];
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

