message.content:
## The Bug

This is the classic **closure variable capture** bug in Go.

All five goroutines close over the **same** variable `i`. The loop finishes (setting `i` to `5`) before the goroutines get a chance to run. When they do execute, they all read the final value `5`.

## The Fix

Pass `i` as an argument to the goroutine so each gets its own **copy** of the value at the time the goroutine is launched:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

Alternatively, you can shadow the variable inside the loop body with `i := i`, but passing as an argument is the most explicit and idiomatic approach.

> **Note:** In Go 1.22+, loop variables are scoped per-iteration, so this bug no longer occurs. But on Go 1.21 you must handle it explicitly.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			fmt.Println(i)
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

