## The Bug

The line `order.shipping_address.city` throws the error because `order.shipping_address` is `undefined` for some orders in production.

This likely happens because not all orders have a shipping address (e.g., digital goods, orders that haven't been fully processed yet, or data inconsistencies). It works on your machine because you're testing with orders that all have a `shipping_address`.

## The Fix

Use **optional chaining** (`?.`) to safely access the property, and provide a fallback value:

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function getShippingZone(order) {
    const city = order.shipping_address?.city;
    const zones = {
        "New York": "east",
        "Los Angeles": "west",
        "Chicago": "central",
    };
    return zones[city] || "standard";
}
</solution>

When `shipping_address` is `undefined`, the optional chaining operator (`?.`) short-circuits and returns `undefined` instead of throwing. That makes `city` be `undefined`, and the final line `zones[undefined] || "standard"` safely falls back to `"standard"`.

